+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard must show committed board changes in up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second. The delivery approach is load-bearing for the rest of the design: change storage, delivery, and browser recovery are designed together around it, so the wrong choice means rework across three nodes. Three approaches can each meet the 15-second freshness goal, and none is forbidden by policy — self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted — so the choice has to be weighed, not derived.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Every option can meet the 15-second freshness goal, so meeting it is background, not a discriminator. The elements that separate the options:
+
+- **Update latency within the goal (major).** All options pass the 15-second floor, but they differ in headroom: sockets and hosted delivery push changes as they commit; polling adds up to the poll interval of lag on every change.
+- **Vendor independence (major).** Recurring fees, provider coupling, and a provider-shaped subscription model in the browser are real costs; avoiding them keeps the board's operation under project control.
+- **Operational burden of delivery (major).** Connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect handling are ongoing work the project must own; a hosted service or stateless polling moves that work off the project's plate.
+- **Store and browser obligations (moderate).** Each approach imposes its own shape downstream: cursor queries for polling, replayable revisions plus reliable publication for sockets, publication reconciliation for hosted delivery; and a browser polling loop, reconnect/catch-up logic, or a provider SDK respectively.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board (or changes since a cursor) every five seconds. The server stays stateless per request: no sessions to track, no publication step. The store answers cursor queries; the browser owns a polling loop; delivery is plain request/response.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency (major): **weak** — every change carries up to five seconds of lag on top of request time; the weakest headroom of the three.
+- Vendor independence (strong): **strong** — no vendor involved at any layer.
+- Operational burden (major): **strong** — stateless request/response; nothing to scale per connection and no reconnect protocol to own.
+- Store and browser obligations (moderate): **partial** — the store needs cursor queries and the browser runs a polling loop, but both are simple and well-understood.
+
+**Summary.** The simplest operation, paid for with repeated reads, seconds of lag, and a polling loop in every browser.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The project runs a WebSocket endpoint. Committed revisions are published to connected browsers over their open sockets; the store keeps replayable revisions so a reconnecting browser can catch up from a cursor; delivery manages connection capacity and session lifecycle; the browser reconnects and resumes after a disconnect.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency (major): **strong** — changes reach browsers as they commit, far inside the 15-second goal.
+- Vendor independence (major): **strong** — no realtime vendor bill and no provider coupling; operation stays under project control.
+- Operational burden (major): **weak** — the project owns connection capacity, reconnect handling, and reliable publication; this is the cost accepted for the other wins.
+- Store and browser obligations (moderate): **partial** — the store needs replayable revisions and a publication outbox, and the browser needs reconnect/catch-up logic; both are real work but self-contained.
+
+**Summary.** Fast updates without a realtime vendor, at the price of owning connection and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A managed realtime provider carries change publication. The store reconciles its committed revisions with the provider's publication; delivery wraps the provider's API and credentials; browsers subscribe using the provider's model.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency (major): **strong** — low-latency push, comparable to self-hosted sockets.
+- Vendor independence (major): **weak** — recurring fees, provider coupling, and a provider-shaped subscription model in the browser.
+- Operational burden (major): **strong** — connection capacity and reconnect machinery are largely the provider's problem.
+- Store and browser obligations (moderate): **partial** — publication reconciliation in the store and a provider SDK in the browser, both keyed to one vendor's semantics.
+
+**Summary.** The quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection operations, at the price of fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** The decision turns on weighing update latency and vendor independence (both major) against operational burden (also major): the project accepts owning connection capacity and reconnect handling because fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor is the better trade for this product. The tenet that self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted is engaged through these criteria — policy does not decide the question, so the weighing does.
+
+Committing forecloses the stateless simplicity of polling and the hands-off operations of a hosted provider. It obligates downstream: the change store keeps replayable revisions and a publication outbox, delivery owns WebSocket sessions and reliable publication, and browser sync owns reconnect and catch-up from a revision cursor — the three nodes sized around this choice.
