 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over WebSockets run by the project. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it is the simplest stateless operation, but it means repeated reads, seconds of lag, cursor queries in the store, and a polling loop in every browser. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it is the quickest route to low-latency updates with less connection operations, but it adds recurring fees and provider coupling, publication reconciliation in the store, and a provider-shaped subscription model in the browser. Self-hosted sockets were chosen because fast updates without a realtime vendor bill outweigh owning connection capacity and reconnect handling. Full write-up: `docs/decisions/self-hosted-websocket-delivery.md`.
